-- #1620 CONTROL — main.mdk with the COLLISION removed and nothing else changed: no
-- `import alpha`, no `impl Alpha T`. One interface, one impl, same data type, same call.
-- The single flipped dimension is whether a SECOND module's interface declares a method
-- with the same name.
--
-- It is graded with the SAME `build-run` verb as main.mdk, so it exercises the entire path
-- this pin blames (front end, emitter, clang, executed binary). A red here means that path
-- broke in general, and the harness says CONTROL-BROKE rather than DRAINED.
--
-- ⚠️ True IS ALSO THE ANSWER A CORRECT COMPILER GIVES FOR main.mdk, and that is deliberate.
-- It makes this control DOUBLE as the post-fix regression guard: selecting by interface
-- identity makes main.mdk print True too, so main.mdk's pinned stdout moves (a clean
-- DRAINED) while this control keeps passing. Posing the control on the buggy value instead
-- is what made #1182's old fixture produce a CONTROL-BROKE whose printed advice ("the
-- ENVIRONMENT moved, not the bug") was wrong -- see that fixture's claim.txt.
--
-- Per #1144 this harness grades a control on its EXIT CODE ONLY, and a
-- wrong-value-at-exit-0 bug cannot be discriminated by an exit code unless the program puts
-- the value INTO the exit code. So `main` ASSERTS True and panics otherwise, rather than
-- printing it — a control that merely printed would exit 0 either way and could never fail.
import beta.{Beta, ping}

data T = T

impl Beta T where
  ping _ = 7

main =
  if ping T == 7 then
    println "control-ok:True"
  else
    panic "control regressed: with no colliding interface, ping T is no longer 7"
